BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315282672@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260822T021616Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260822T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260822T140000
SUMMARY:The History and Geology of the Scarborough Bluffs!
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the 
 following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nWelcome to Tor
 onto History Walks! This walking tour blends the human history and the nat
 ural history of the area. A fascinating tour\, so don't miss it. If you ar
 e new to Toronto then this tour is for you!\n\nThe Scarborough Bluffs is T
 oronto's best kept secret. Known as "Les Grands Ecores." by the French\, a
 nd in 1788 Alexander Aitken\, called the bluffs the High Lands. A hidden g
 em in Toronto which hides many secrets.\n\nElizabeth Simcoe named the Bluf
 fs the Scarborough Highlands in 1793 and had reminded Elizabeth Simcoe of 
 the limestone cliffs back in England\, in her own words from her diary:\n\
 n"The shore is extremely bold and has the appearance of chalk cliffs\, but
  I believe they are only white sand. They appeared so well that we talked 
 of building a summer residence there and calling it Scarborough."\n\nThe S
 carborough Bluffs extended west along the coastline of Lake Ontario toward
 s the town of York and later Toronto\, but vast areas along the western ed
 ge were razed for industrial and residential development. The formation of
  the chalk cliffs continues to shrink due to erosion.\n\nThe price of this
  tour is $15.00 per person\n\nThis extensive walking tour begins at Bluffe
 rs Park Road and Brimley Road South\, south of Kingston Road near the lake
  at the marina stop!\n\nThe 201 Bluffers Park Bus leaves Kennedy Subway St
 ation every 15 minutes.\n\nHope to see you there!\n\n\\*\\* **Please note:
  Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour
 ! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@g
 mail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315282
 672/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260616T121041Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260616T121041Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315569426@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260822T021616Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260829T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260829T140000
SUMMARY:The History of the Eaton Centre (NEW for 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Explorer Pa
 ss is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, em
 ail**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for si
 gning up!**\n\nMost people visit Toronto's Eaton Centre to shop\, and it's
  a great place to do just that. But few realize they are walking through o
 ne of the city's greatest stories of ambition\, controversy\, architecture
 \, and change.\n\nJoin me as we step beyond the storefronts to uncover the
  remarkable history hidden beneath the soaring glass roof of the galleria.
  Discover how this world famous complex rose from a maze of Victorian stre
 ets\, warehouses\, theatres\, and historic buildings\, and learn why one o
 f Toronto's largest redevelopment projects almost resulted in the demoliti
 on of Old City Hall and the beloved Church of the Holy Trinity.\n\nThis fa
 scinating walking tour explores nearly 160 years of Toronto history\, begi
 nning with the extraordinary rise of Timothy Eaton and the department stor
 e that transformed shopping into the "art of shopping\," almost overnight.
 \n\nWe'll examine the neighbourhood that existed before the Eaton Centre\,
  meet some of the colourful characters who worked and lived here\, and tra
 ce the dramatic battles between developers\, preservationists\, politician
 s\, and citizens that forever changed the face of downtown Toronto. Along 
 the way\, you'll see surviving landmarks\, forgotten streets\, hidden arch
 itectural secrets\, and public art that millions pass every year without n
 oticing.\n\nYou'll also discover why the Eaton Centre became an internatio
 nal model for shopping centre design\, admire its spectacular vaulted glas
 s ceiling\, and hear the stories behind the famous flock of suspended Cana
 da geese.\n\nWe'll explore how the complex evolved through the decline of 
 the Eaton's department store\, and the arrival of new retailers\, that hel
 ped redefine the downtown core. From engineering marvels to architectural 
 triumphs\, this is the story of how a shopping mall became one of Canada's
  most recognizable landmarks.\n\nWhether you're a lifelong Torontonian\, a
  first-time visitor\, a lover of architecture\, or someone fascinated by u
 rban history\, this tour offers a new perspective on a place you thought y
 ou knew. By the end of our walk\, you'll never look at the Eaton Centre th
 e same way again\, I promise. Come and discover the hidden history beneath
  the glass canopy\, and experience one of Toronto's most compelling storie
 s.\n\nThis event is for all levels and walkers\, there are a few steps her
 e and there to navigate\, but mostly on one level.\n\nThis walking tour be
 gins on the steps of Old City Hall at the northeast corner of Bay Street a
 nd Queen Street West\, and\nhas a ticket price of $15.00 per person.\n\n**
 Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space f
 or this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohi
 storywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315569
 426/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260706T115742Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260706T115742Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315028619@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260822T021616Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260905T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260905T140000
SUMMARY:Landmarks 4: The British Coffee House to Victoria Square (NEW)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Yearly Pass
  is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, emai
 l**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for sign
 ing up!**\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount whe
 n booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following em
 ail at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nIn this new volume\, Landm
 arks 4 revisits the site of some of Toronto's urban legends\, and historic
  buildings that gave rise to the city. This walk's focus is on the King St
 reet West\, Portland Street and lower Bathurst Street area\n\nFrom there w
 e wander around the area taking in landmarks not otherwise explored on oth
 er Toronto History Walks tours. So get your brains and feet ready for a wo
 rkout my friends.\n\nThe British coffee house at the corner of King Street
  West and York Street in the old Chewett building dates from the 1830s. Ho
 wever\, after the demise of the coffee house it became one of Toronto's be
 st hotels the Rossin House.\n\nThere is also a connection to one of Toront
 o's best known characters\, John Howard of High Park fame and the provinci
 al Lunatic Asylum. It was in this building that John Howard lived until hi
 s country home out in High Park was completed.\n\nVictoria Square is a lit
 tle known gem in the heart of Toronto\, and some of our earliest history b
 egins there. So this tour returns walkers to the time period between 1812 
 and 1880 and everything in between.\n\nToronto is a city that hides its hi
 story in plain sight. Beneath the glass towers are layers of stories — g
 rand hotels that vanished overnight\, political scandals whispered through
  stone corridors\, theatres that once glittered under gaslight\, and lanew
 ays where the city first learned how to become a metropolis.\n\nThis new T
 oronto History Walks series of 60 different walking tours invites the walk
 er to slow down\, and see the city differently.\n\nOver the course of 30 c
 arefully chosen stops\, this walking tour traces the landmarks that helped
  shape the history of Toronto. Beginning at the site of the legendary Holl
 and House — once one of the city’s finest mansions\, and a gathering p
 lace for politicians\, and businessmen\, the routes wind through the city 
 uncovering stories both famous and forgotten.\n\nSome locations are grand 
 and imposing. Others are easy to walk past without a second glance. Every 
 stop reveals a piece of Toronto’s personality. At Osgoode Hall\, visitor
 s will stand before one of the city’s most elegant historic buildings. N
 earby\, the old banks and newspaper buildings of the financial district re
 call an era when Toronto proudly called itself “Toronto the Good\,” ev
 en as corruption and backroom dealings simmered beneath the surface.\n\nTh
 e tour also explores the vanished city — the Toronto that survives only 
 in photographs\, and memory. Walkers will discover where magnificent Victo
 rian structures once stood before demolition crews erased entire blocks in
  the name of progress. Former theatres\, hotels\, markets\, and department
  stores emerge again through storytelling\, helping modern explorers imagi
 ne the bustle\, smoke\, and noise of earlier generations.\n\nStories of fi
 re\, disaster\, celebration\, and reinvention. Participants will hear how 
 the Great Fire of 1904 transformed downtown streets\, how immigrant commun
 ities reshaped neighbourhoods block by block\, and how Toronto repeatedly 
 reinvented itself through booms\, depressions\, wars\, and waves of newcom
 ers.\n\nBut this tour is not simply about dates and buildings. It is about
  people. The politicians who argued over Confederation. The hotel clerks w
 ho witnessed scandals. The newspaper editors who shaped public opinion. Th
 e shopkeepers\, labourers\, entertainers\, and ordinary Torontonians whose
  lives unfolded along these same streets.\n\nToronto has always had hidden
  corners and strange tales: tunnels beneath streets\, forgotten cemeteries
  beneath office towers\, unsolved crimes\, and buildings said to carry ech
 oes of the past. Even longtime residents may find themselves surprised by 
 what has been quietly standing before them all along. Come along and exper
 ience a very different city. A deep dive into our history.\n\nThis walking
  tour begins at the southeast **corner** of King Street West and York Stre
 et\, just ignore the entire 11th floor that's a flaw in the directions app
  within Meetup\, I will be on the ground floor\, trust me! We end our tour
  at Victoria Square near Portland and Wellington Street.\n\nThis walking t
 our is for all walking levels. Please dress for the season\, and hope to s
 ee you there.\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per person.\n\n\\*
 \\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your s
 pace for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**tor
 ontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315028
 619/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260530T193230Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260530T193230Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315367640@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260822T021616Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260911T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260911T140000
SUMMARY:Ireland Park\, Queen's Wharf\, and 1847 Irish Potato Famine
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Explo
 rer Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tour
 s\, email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or 
 for signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the tic
 ket amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to th
 e following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nThis special
  historical walking tour discusses the Irish Potato Famine in great detail
 \, and the implications for Toronto in 1847.\n\nStarting the walking tour 
 near Ireland Park just east of Bathurst Quay we take a deep dive into the 
 events that saw thousands of people head west to Toronto in 1847! A truly 
 global event! A life changing event for those caught up in the famine in I
 reland and Toronto itself. Toronto was never the same after\, and impacted
  the future of the city for nearly a century.\n\nBut the stories surroundi
 ng Ireland Park are not the only stories from this era. This tour looks at
  the Potato Famine with a different lens. We end at the corner of King and
  John streets at the site of the Fever Sheds.\n\nThis extensive tour begin
 s in **Little Norway Park** adjacent to Queen's Quay West\, at the foot of
  Bathurst Street\, and ends at King Street and John Street!\n\nThis tour i
 s for all walking levels. Please dress for the season.\n\nThis walk has a 
 ticket price of $15.00 per person!\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-trans
 fer the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-tra
 nsfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315367
 640/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260622T133845Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260622T133845Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315029719@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260822T021616Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260912T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260912T150000
SUMMARY:Lights\, Camera\, Action: Hollywood North Film Locations!
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Explo
 rer Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tour
 s\, email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or 
 for signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the tic
 ket price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the
  following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\n**It's that t
 ime again everyone\, and TIFF and the stars are back in Toronto as if they
  ever left. So let's do another film locations extravaganza and have some 
 fun in the mean time. I love this tour.**\n\nMary Pickford stares into the
  camera.\n\nBorn on University Avenue\, she became the girl with the golde
 n curls\, the first Hollywood star\, and now Toronto is the star!\n\nGood 
 Will Hunting\, Pacific Rim\, X-Men\, Robocop\, Total Recall\, Prom Night\,
  Suicide Squad\, My Big Fat Greek Wedding\, Police Academy\, and the list 
 goes on and on and on.\n\nIt was back in the 1920s that Toronto emerged as
  a shy newcomer. But it stepped up to the camera\, smiled\, and went to wo
 rk. The first real feature movie was "The Man From Toronto\," even though 
 it was not filmed here\, and in the late 60s Toronto was "Goin' Down the R
 oad\," that the city was Hollywood bound.\n\nAnd it wasn't just movies! Te
 levision movies\, series\, documentaries\, music videos and video games we
 re all shot here in Toronto. Award shows such as The Genie's\, Juno's\, th
 e Canadian Screen Awards\, Giller Prize and The Much Music Video Awards ar
 e all celebrated here! Let's find out where the movies came alive with thi
 s unique walking tour!\n\nThis walking tour is for all walking levels and 
 fitness skills. Please dress for the season and bring water. And remember 
 to bring your love of movie magic as well. Hope to see you there.\n\nThis 
 tour begins at the famous TORONTO sign at Nathan Phillips Square. We end a
 t Front and Jarvis!\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per person!\
 n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket price when booking y
 our space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n
 **torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315029
 719/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260530T212705Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260530T212705Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315185584@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260822T021616Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260913T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260913T140000
SUMMARY:On the Trail of the Family Compact (NEW for 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Explo
 rer Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tour
 s\, email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or 
 for signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the tic
 ket price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the
  following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nThe **Family 
 Compact** was a small group of powerful men who controlled much of the pol
 itical\, and social life in what was then called Upper Canada (Ontario) du
 ring the early 1800s. They were not an official organization with membersh
 ip cards or meetings\, but rather a close-knit network of politicians\, ju
 dges\, church leaders\, and wealthy businessmen who often shared similar b
 ackgrounds\, and family connections.\n\nBecause so many important position
 s were held by members of this circle\, they had enormous influence over g
 overnment decisions\, land distribution\, banking\, education\, and policy
 . To many ordinary settlers arriving in the colony\, it often seemed as th
 ough a handful of well-connected families were running the province.\n\nTh
 e influence of the Family Compact was strong in the growing town of York\,
  later Toronto. York was the colonial capital\, and many of the colony's m
 ost powerful figures lived and worked there. Men such as John Strachan pla
 yed major roles in shaping the colony's institutions. The group believed t
 hat society worked best when guided by educated and wealthy leaders\, and 
 they often looked to Britain as their model.\n\nWhile they saw themselves 
 as protecting stability and order\, critics argued that they were protecti
 ng their own privileges and limiting opportunities for others.\n\nAs Upper
  Canada grew\, dissatisfaction with the Family Compact also grew. Farmers\
 , merchants\, and reform-minded citizens became frustrated by what they sa
 w as favoritism\, and limited political representation. Many believed that
  government positions and economic opportunities were being distributed ba
 sed on connections rather than merit. Reformers such as William Lyon Macke
 nzie used newspapers and public meetings to challenge the Compact's power 
 and demand a more democratic system of government.\n\nThe conflict reached
  a dramatic point during the Upper Canada Rebellion of 1837. Although the 
 rebellion itself was quickly defeated\, it highlighted widespread concerns
  about how the colony was being governed. In the years that followed\, pol
 itical reforms gradually reduced the influence of the Family Compact and i
 ntroduced more responsible government.\n\nToday\, the Family Compact is re
 membered as an important part of Toronto and Ontario's early history\, and
  how growing demands for democracy helped shape the province we know today
 .\n\nThis tour explores some of the sites associated with the Family Compa
 ct\, and the history of early Toronto and Ontario. There are about 25 stop
 s on this new tour for 2026!\n\nThis walk begins and ends outside St. Jame
 s Cathedral at 106 King Street East at Church Street.\n\nThis tour has a t
 icketed price of $15.00 per person!\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-tran
 sfer the ticket price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-tra
 nsfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315185
 584/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260609T172100Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260609T172100Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315583586@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260822T021616Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260913T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260913T170000
SUMMARY:Historic Buildings Walking Tour!
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Explo
 rer Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tour
 s\, email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or 
 for signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the tic
 ket amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to th
 e following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nToronto has 
 a rich history\, and it's all around us\, but certain buildings do stand o
 ut and tell our story. On this walking tour the following buildings are ex
 plored and their history revealed. This new tour is for residents and tour
 ists who want to reconnect with their city\, or get to know it from a diff
 erent vantage point!\n\n* Daniel Brooke Building\n* St. Lawrence Hall\n* S
 t. James Anglican Cathedral\n* St. Michael's Cathedral\n* Mackenzie House\
 n* Massey Hall\n* Old City Hall\n* Osgoode Hall\n* Campbell House Museum\n
 * Gooderham Building\n\nand a few other hidden surprises along the way fro
 m the Toronto Stock Exchange to the New City Hall and a few others in betw
 een!\n\nThis walking tour is for all walking levels. Please dress for the 
 season\, and hope to see you there.\n\nPlease meet in front of the iconic 
 St. Lawrence Hall at the corner of Jarvis and King Street East\, we end th
 e tour at Historic Campbell House!\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.
 00 per person.\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amoun
 t when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the followi
 ng email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315583
 586/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260707T111412Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260707T111412Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
