BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_314965349@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260822T021616Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260919T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260919T140000
SUMMARY:History of Aviation: Air Wars (LECTURE ON THE GO)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Explorer Pa
 ss is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, em
 ail**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for si
 gning up!**\n\nWe have an amazing "Lecture on the Go" series for 2026. New
  topics and fascinating stories abound! Join us and learn about the world 
 and keep active and warm and cool during the winter and summer months!\n\n
 I started this walking classroom when we were under that horrible cloud ca
 lled Covid. This series was popular and helped people get back into societ
 y and life. **This is part two of the Aviation series that will debut in A
 ugust 2026 with the Lecture on the disaster at Tenerife**!\n\nThe "Lecture
  on the Go" Series is open to members who have purchased the YEARLY MEMBER
 SHIP PASS\, it's free for those members. If you wish to attend a lecture a
 nd you are not a yearly pass member then the price **per** lecture is $20.
 00.\n\nIn this second part of *History of Aviation: Lecture on the Go* ser
 ies\, we move from the daring pioneers of early flight into the era when a
 viation truly changed North America forever. By the 1920s and 1930s\, airp
 lanes were no longer just fragile machines flown by adventurers. Air mail 
 routes connected distant cities\, passenger airlines began appearing acros
 s the continent\, and aircraft designers pushed technology forward at a br
 eathtaking pace.\n\nThe skies over North America became a laboratory of in
 novation\, where speed\, distance\, and reliability improved year after ye
 ar. It was an exciting time filled with record-breaking flights\, famous p
 ilots\, and the growing belief that aviation would shape the future.\n\nTh
 e Second World War transformed aviation more dramatically than anyone coul
 d have imagined. Aircraft became central to modern warfare\, leading to wh
 at many historians call the first true “air wars.” Massive bomber flee
 ts crossed oceans\, fighter aircraft battled for control of the skies\, an
 d radar and engine technology advanced at incredible speed. Companies like
  Boeing and Lockheed Martin grew alongside the military demand for aircraf
 t production. Air power became a decisive force\, changing military strate
 gy forever. Yet even during wartime\, aviation inspired people with storie
 s of courage\, skill\, and technological achievement that captured the ima
 gination of the public across North America.\n\nCanada also played a remar
 kably important role in the growth of aviation during this era. The Canadi
 an aviation industry expanded rapidly during the war years\, building trai
 ning schools\, manufacturing aircraft\, and supporting Allied operations a
 cross the Atlantic. Programs such as the British Commonwealth Air Training
  Plan turned Canada into one of the world’s great pilot-training countri
 es. Canadian companies like de Havilland Canada became known for designing
  rugged aircraft capable of operating in harsh northern conditions. Bush p
 lanes and bush pilots like Max Ward (Wardair) opened remote regions of the
  country\, connecting isolated communities and supporting exploration\, mi
 ning\, and medical transport. Aviation helped shape modern Canada in ways 
 that many people today rarely stop to consider.\n\nAfter the war\, aviatio
 n entered the jet age and North America became the focus of global aerospa
 ce innovation. Commercial air travel exploded in popularity as families co
 uld suddenly cross the continent in hours rather than days. Airports expan
 ded into enormous transportation hubs\, while military aviation evolved in
 to the era of supersonic fighters\, strategic bombers\, and space explorat
 ion. The story of aviation in North America is ultimately a story of ambit
 ion\, and invention\, a reminder that humanity has always looked toward th
 e sky and imagined what might come next. But this story has plenty of myst
 ery and thrills\, so come aboard and take to the skies. So if you love to 
 fly then this is your boarding pass!\n\nIn this lecture on the Go\,\nUpcom
 ing topics in the 2026 series:\n\n\\*Philosophy 1: Great Minds of the West
 ern World: Goethe to Kant! - (August 2026)\n\\*Philosophy 2: Great Minds o
 f the Eastern\nWorld: Chuang Tzu to Dalai Lama - (July 2026)\nThe 1977 Dis
 aster at Tenerife - (August 2026)\nLegacy of Triangle: How a New York Fact
 ory Scandal changed North America - (September 2026)\nHistory of Aviation 
 (September 2026)\n\nSo book your plans now!\n\nThis event is for all level
 s and walkers\, there are a few steps here and there to navigate\, but mos
 tly on one level. This event in a non-walking event.\n\nThe lecture begins
  on the steps of Old City Hall at the northeast corner of Bay Street and Q
 ueen Street West\, and\nhas a ticket price of $20.00 per person\, but **fr
 ee with the yearly membership pass**.\n\n**Please note: Please e-transfer 
 the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfe
 r to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
